Q900 UEFI Secure Boot keys
========================

Run these commands from the NAOS repository root on a Linux build host with
OpenSSL and sbsigntool installed. This example creates one RSA certificate
for lab testing and uses it for all three UEFI variables.

    (
        set -eu
        umask 077
        key_dir="$PWD/key/qcom-uefi-secure-boot"
        mkdir -p "$key_dir"
        for file in db.key db.crt PK.auth KEK.auth db.auth; do
            if [ -e "$key_dir/$file" ] || [ -L "$key_dir/$file" ]; then
                printf 'Signing material already exists at %s\n' "$key_dir/$file" >&2
                exit 1
            fi
        done

        work_dir=$(mktemp -d)
        trap 'rm -rf -- "$work_dir"' EXIT
        openssl req -new -x509 -newkey rsa:3072 -sha256 -nodes -days 365 \
            -subj '/CN=NAOS Q900 UEFI development' \
            -keyout "$work_dir/db.key" -out "$work_dir/db.crt"
        openssl x509 -in "$work_dir/db.crt" -outform DER -out "$work_dir/db.der"
        sbsiglist --owner "$(cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/uuid)" --type x509 \
            --output "$work_dir/db.esl" "$work_dir/db.der"

        attrs=NON_VOLATILE,BOOTSERVICE_ACCESS,RUNTIME_ACCESS,TIME_BASED_AUTHENTICATED_WRITE_ACCESS
        for var in PK KEK db; do
            sbvarsign --attr "$attrs" --key "$work_dir/db.key" \
                --cert "$work_dir/db.crt" --output "$work_dir/$var.auth" \
                "$var" "$work_dir/db.esl"
        done

        install -m 0600 "$work_dir/db.key" "$key_dir/db.key"
        for file in db.crt PK.auth KEK.auth db.auth; do
            install -m 0644 "$work_dir/$file" "$key_dir/$file"
        done
    )

The build uses db.key and db.crt for signing EFI images. The factory boot
partition carries PK.auth, KEK.auth and db.auth for enrollment. Keep db.key on
the signing host. For separate production certificates, sign PK.auth with PK,
KEK.auth with PK, and db.auth with KEK. Use the db signing certificate and its
private key as db.crt and db.key.

With the production build inputs configured, build the signed factory image.

    kas build kas/production-qcom.yml

The Q900 production profile keeps enrollment manual. Firmware 00569 reported
successful enrollment, then returned to Setup Mode with PK, KEK and db absent
after reboot. This can cause an automatic enrollment loop.

After enrollment, power-cycle the device and verify that SecureBoot is 1,
SetupMode is 0, and PK, KEK and db are still present.
